
Understanding the Problem Before the Solution
Procedural programming works. For small programs, it is the right choice. 
But as programs grow, procedural code becomes difficult to manage. This chapter shows why that happens and how object-oriented programming solves those problems.

1.1 The Procedural Programming Model
Procedural programming organizes code around functions. Data is stored in variables. 
Functions operate on that data. Below is a complete banking program written 
procedurally. It handles one account.

cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

// Global variables for a single account
string accountOwner = "";
double accountBalance = 0.0;

void createAccount(string owner) {
    accountOwner = owner;
    accountBalance = 0.0;
    cout << "Account created for " << owner << endl;
}

void deposit(double amount) {
    accountBalance = accountBalance + amount;
    cout << "Deposited " << amount << ". New balance: " << accountBalance << endl;
}

bool withdraw(double amount) {
    if (amount <= accountBalance) {
        accountBalance = accountBalance - amount;
        cout << "Withdrew " << amount << ". New balance: " << accountBalance << endl;
        return true;
    } else {
        cout << "Insufficient funds" << endl;
        return false;
    }
}

double getBalance() {
    return accountBalance;
}

int main() {
    createAccount("Alice");
    deposit(100.0);
    withdraw(30.0);
    cout << "Final balance: " << getBalance() << endl;
    return 0;
}
Output:

text
Account created for Alice
Deposited 100. New balance: 100
Withdrew 30. New balance: 70
Final balance: 70
The code works. Each function modifies the global accountBalance. The functions and data are separate but connected through global variables. This is simple and readable.

1.2 The Breaking Point
Now add a second account. Without classes, the programmer must duplicate 
variables and pass them carefully to each function.

cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

// Account 1
string account1Owner = "";
double account1Balance = 0.0;

// Account 2
string account2Owner = "";
double account2Balance = 0.0;

void createAccount(int accountNum, string owner) {
    if (accountNum == 1) {
        account1Owner = owner;
        account1Balance = 0.0;
        cout << "Account 1 created for " << owner << endl;
    } else {
        account2Owner = owner;
        account2Balance = 0.0;
        cout << "Account 2 created for " << owner << endl;
    }
}

void deposit(int accountNum, double amount) {
    if (accountNum == 1) {
        account1Balance = account1Balance + amount;
        cout << "Deposited " << amount << " to account 1. Balance: " << account1Balance << endl;
    } else {
        account2Balance = account2Balance + amount;
        cout << "Deposited " << amount << " to account 2. Balance: " << account2Balance << endl;
    }
}

bool withdraw(int accountNum, double amount) {
    if (accountNum == 1) {
        if (amount <= account1Balance) {
            account1Balance = account1Balance - amount;
            cout << "Withdrew " << amount << " from account 1. Balance: " << account1Balance << endl;
            return true;
        }
    } else {
        if (amount <= account2Balance) {
            account2Balance = account2Balance - amount;
            cout << "Withdrew " << amount << " from account 2. Balance: " << account2Balance << endl;
            return true;
        }
    }
    cout << "Insufficient funds" << endl;
    return false;
}

double getBalance(int accountNum) {
    if (accountNum == 1) {
        return account1Balance;
    } else {
        return account2Balance;
    }
}

int main() {
    createAccount(1, "Alice");
    createAccount(2, "Bob");
    
    deposit(1, 100.0);
    deposit(2, 50.0);
    
    withdraw(1, 30.0);
    
    cout << "Alice balance: " << getBalance(1) << endl;
    cout << "Bob balance: " << getBalance(2) << endl;
    
    return 0;
}
The problems are immediate:

Every function needs an accountNum parameter

Adding a third account means adding account3Owner, account3Balance, and modifying every function

The programmer must track which balance belongs with which owner

Functions are disconnected from the data they operate on

Code duplication grows with each new account

This is the breaking point. At this scale, procedural programming becomes unmanageable.

1.3 Introducing the Class
A class is a blueprint that bundles data with the functions that operate on that 
data. The data becomes member variables. The functions become member functions 
(also called methods).

Below is the banking program rewritten as a class.

cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class BankAccount {
private:
    string owner;
    double balance;
    
public:
    // Constructor - runs when a new account is created
    BankAccount(string accountOwner) {
        owner = accountOwner;
        balance = 0.0;
        cout << "Account created for " << owner << endl;
    }
    
    void deposit(double amount) {
        balance = balance + amount;
        cout << "Deposited " << amount << " to " << owner;
        cout << ". Balance: " << balance << endl;
    }
    
    bool withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount <= balance) {
            balance = balance - amount;
            cout << "Withdrew " << amount << " from " << owner;
            cout << ". Balance: " << balance << endl;
            return true;
        } else {
            cout << "Insufficient funds for " << owner << endl;
            return false;
        }
    }
    
    double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
    
    string getOwner() {
        return owner;
    }
};

int main() {
    BankAccount alice("Alice");
    BankAccount bob("Bob");
    
    alice.deposit(100.0);
    bob.deposit(50.0);
    
    alice.withdraw(30.0);
    
    cout << alice.getOwner() << " balance: " << alice.getBalance() << endl;
    cout << bob.getOwner() << " balance: " << bob.getBalance() << endl;
    
    return 0;
}
Output:

text
Account created for Alice
Account created for Bob
Deposited 100 to Alice. Balance: 100
Deposited 50 to Bob. Balance: 50
Withdrew 30 from Alice. Balance: 70
Alice balance: 70
Bob balance: 50
Explanation of each part:

Syntax	Meaning
class BankAccount { ... };	Creates a new class named BankAccount. 
Note the semicolon after the closing brace.
private:	Members inside this section cannot be accessed from outside the class
public:	Members inside this section can be accessed from anywhere
string owner; double balance;	Member variables (data) belonging to each BankAccount object
BankAccount(string accountOwner)	Constructor – runs automatically when a new object is created
balance = 0.0;	Initializes the member variable
void deposit(double amount)	A member function that operates on the object's data
The member functions can directly access owner and balance without any global 
variables. Each BankAccount object has its own copy of owner and balance.

1.4 Creating and Using Objects
An object (also called an instance) is one specific entity created from a class. 
Creating an object is calling the constructor.

cpp
int main() {
    // Creating two separate objects
    BankAccount account1("Alice");   // Object 1
    BankAccount account2("Bob");     // Object 2
    
    // Each object has its own balance
    account1.deposit(100);   // Alice's balance becomes 100
    account2.deposit(50);    // Bob's balance becomes 50
    
    // The dot operator accesses member functions
    cout << account1.getBalance() << endl;  // 100
    cout << account2.getBalance() << endl;  // 50
    
    return 0;
}
Output:

text
Account created for Alice
Account created for Bob
Deposited 100 to Alice. Balance: 100
Deposited 50 to Bob. Balance: 50
100
50
Each object maintains its own data. The class defines the blueprint once. Many objects can be created from that blueprint.

1.5 Key Vocabulary
Term	Definition	Example
Class	A blueprint that defines data and behavior	class BankAccount { ... };
Object	A specific entity created from a class	BankAccount alice("Alice");
Instance	Another word for object	alice is an instance of BankAccount
Member Variable	A variable declared inside a class	double balance;
Member Function	A function declared inside a class	void deposit(double amount)
Constructor	A special member function that initializes new objects	BankAccount(string owner)
1.6 Chapter Summary
Procedural programming separates data from functions, causing confusion when multiple similar items exist

Adding a second account in procedural code requires duplicating variables and modifying every function

A class bundles data (member variables) with behavior (member functions)

The private: section hides data from outside access

The public: section exposes the interface that other code can use

A constructor runs automatically when an object is created

The dot operator (.) accesses member functions of an object

Many objects can be created from one class, each with its own data

