Exercise 3: Create a class Counter with a private member variable count. 
The constructor should set count to 0. Add member functions 
increment(), decrement(), reset(), and getCount(). 
The decrement function should not allow negative counts 
(if count is 0, decrement does nothing).

Solution:

cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Counter {
private:
    int count;
    
public:
    Counter() {
        count = 0;
    }
    
    void increment() {
        count = count + 1;
        cout << "Count: " << count << endl;
    }
    
    void decrement() {
        if (count > 0) {
            count = count - 1;
        }
        cout << "Count: " << count << endl;
    }
    
    void reset() {
        count = 0;
        cout << "Count reset to 0" << endl;
    }
    
    int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
};

int main() {
    Counter c;
    
    c.increment();  // Count: 1
    c.increment();  // Count: 2
    c.decrement();  // Count: 1
    c.decrement();  // Count: 0
    c.decrement();  // Count: 0 (no negative)
    c.reset();      // Count reset to 0
    
    return 0;
}
Chapter 2: Classes – Basics
Writing Your First C++ Class
Creating a class in C++ requires attention to syntax details that differ from other languages. This chapter covers the fundamental building blocks.

2.1 Class Syntax in C++
A class definition has three required parts: 
the class keyword, the class body inside braces, 
and a semicolon after the closing brace.

cpp
class ClassName {
    // Member variables and functions go here
};  // Semicolon is REQUIRED
The most common beginner error is forgetting the semicolon.

cpp
// WRONG - missing semicolon
class Dog {
    string name;
}

// RIGHT
class Dog {
    string name;
};  // Semicolon here
Class members are organized into sections using access specifiers.

cpp
class BankAccount {
private:
    // Only member functions of this class can access these
    double balance;
    
public:
    // Any code can access these
    void deposit(double amount);
};
Multiple access specifiers can appear in any order. The same specifier can appear multiple times.

2.2 Member Variables (Attributes)
Member variables store the data for each object. 
By convention, member variables are placed in the private: section.

Naming conventions for member variables:

Convention	Example	Notes
m_ prefix	m_balance, m_owner	Common in many C++ codebases
Trailing underscore	balance_, owner_	Used in Google style guide
No prefix (but different from parameters)	balance, owner	Relies on this-> or naming discipline
This book uses the trailing underscore convention. It clearly distinguishes member variables from local variables and function parameters.

cpp
class Person {
private:
    string name_;    // Trailing underscore indicates member variable
    int age_;
    
public:
    void setName(string name) {
        name_ = name;   // Clear distinction: parameter 'name', member 'name_'
    }
};
2.3 Member Functions (Methods)
Member functions can be defined inside the class body. Inside a member function, member variables are accessed directly by name.

cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Dog {
private:
    string name_;
    int age_;
    
public:
    void setName(string name) {
        name_ = name;
    }
    
    void setAge(int age) {
        age_ = age;
    }
    
    void bark() {
        cout << name_ << " says Woof!" << endl;
    }
    
    void introduce() {
        cout << "I am " << name_ << ", age " << age_ << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Dog fido;
    fido.setName("Fido");
    fido.setAge(3);
    fido.bark();        // Fido says Woof!
    fido.introduce();   // I am Fido, age 3
    
    return 0;
}
Member functions have direct access to the member variables of 
the object they are called on. When fido.bark() executes, 
name_ refers to fido.name_.

2.4 The Constructor
A constructor is a special member function that runs automatically 
when an object is created. It has the same name as the class and 
no return type (not even void).

cpp
class Student {
private:
    string name_;
    int grade_;
    
public:
    // Constructor - same name as class, no return type
    Student(string name, int grade) {
        name_ = name;
        grade_ = grade;
        cout << "Student created: " << name_ << endl;
    }
    
    void display() {
        cout << name_ << " has grade " << grade_ << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student alice("Alice", 85);  // Constructor runs here
    Student bob("Bob", 92);      // Constructor runs here
    
    alice.display();  // Alice has grade 85
    bob.display();    // Bob has grade 92
    
    return 0;
}
Default constructor: A constructor that takes no 
parameters. If no constructor is written, the compiler 
provides an empty default constructor (which does nothing).

cpp
class Simple {
    // Compiler provides: Simple() { }
};

class WithCustom {
public:
    // Custom constructor - compiler no longer provides default
    WithCustom(int value) {
        // ...
    }
};

int main() {
    Simple s;           // OK - compiler-generated default constructor
    // WithCustom w;    // ERROR - no default constructor available
    WithCustom w(10);   // OK - custom constructor exists
    
    return 0;
}
Constructor initializer list 
(preferred over assignment inside the constructor body):

cpp
class Student {
private:
    string name_;
    int grade_;
    
public:
    // Initializer list - more efficient, required for const members
    Student(string name, int grade) : name_(name), grade_(grade) {
        // Constructor body - usually empty or minimal
        cout << "Student created: " << name_ << endl;
    }
};
The initializer list runs before the constructor body. 
It is the preferred way to initialize member variables.

2.5 Creating Objects
Objects are created on the stack (not using new) for now. 
Stack allocation is simple and automatic.

cpp
class Car {
public:
    string model_;
    int year_;
    
    void display() {
        cout << year_ << " " << model_ << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    // Stack allocation - object created directly
    Car myCar;           // Default constructor runs
    myCar.model_ = "Toyota";
    myCar.year_ = 2022;
    
    // Another object
    Car yourCar;
    yourCar.model_ = "Honda";
    yourCar.year_ = 2023;
    
    myCar.display();    // 2022 Toyota
    yourCar.display();  // 2023 Honda
    
    return 0;
}
The dot operator (.) accesses members of an object.

Syntax	Meaning
object.memberVariable	Access a member variable
object.memberFunction()	Call a member function
2.6 Getters and Setters
Getters (accessors) provide read-only access to private member variables. They are marked const because they do not modify the object.

Setters (mutators) provide controlled write access to private member variables. They can validate input before changing the value.

cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Product {
private:
    string name_;
    double price_;
    
public:
    Product(string name, double price) : name_(name), price_(price) {}
    
    // Getter - const means this function does not modify the object
    string getName() const {
        return name_;
    }
    
    double getPrice() const {
        return price_;
    }
    
    // Setter with validation
    void setPrice(double newPrice) {
        if (newPrice >= 0) {
            price_ = newPrice;
        } else {
            cout << "Error: Price cannot be negative" << endl;
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    Product laptop("Laptop", 999.99);
    
    cout << "Product: " << laptop.getName() << endl;      // Laptop
    cout << "Price: $" << laptop.getPrice() << endl;      // 999.99
    
    laptop.setPrice(1099.99);
    cout << "New price: $" << laptop.getPrice() << endl;  // 1099.99
    
    laptop.setPrice(-50);  // Error: Price cannot be negative
    
    return 0;
}
The const keyword on member functions promises that the function will not modify any member variables. A const member function can be called on const objects.

cpp
class Example {
private:
    int value_;
    
public:
    int getValue() const {
        return value_;   // OK - reading only
    }
    
    void setValue(int v) {
        value_ = v;      // OK - modifies, so cannot be const
    }
};

int main() {
    const Example e;     // const object
    // e.setValue(10);   // ERROR - cannot call non-const function on const object
    cout << e.getValue(); // OK - getValue is const
    
    return 0;
}
2.7 Chapter Summary
A class definition requires a semicolon after the closing brace

Member variables are typically private: and use a naming convention (this book uses trailing underscore)

Member functions are typically public: and define the class interface

A constructor has the same name as the class and no return type

The constructor initializer list (colon syntax) is preferred over assignment in the constructor body

Objects are created on the stack with simple declaration syntax

The dot operator (.) accesses object members

Getters provide read access and should be const

Setters provide controlled write access with optional validation

2.8 Check Your Understanding
Question 1: What is the most common syntax error when writing a class in C++?

Answer: Forgetting the semicolon after the closing brace of the class definition.

Question 2: What is the difference between a member variable and a local variable?

Answer: A member variable belongs to an object and persists for the lifetime of 
the object. A local variable exists only while the function is executing.

Question 3: Why are getters marked with const?

Answer: To indicate that the function does not modify the object, 
and to allow the function to be called on const objects.

Question 4: What is a constructor initializer list?

Answer: A list after the colon in a constructor definition that initializes 
member variables before the constructor body runs.

Question 5: What happens if a class has a custom constructor and no default constructor?

Answer: The compiler no longer provides a default constructor. Objects of that class must be created with arguments matching the custom constructor.

