Exercise 3: Create a class Thermostat with a private member variable temperature_ (in Celsius). The constructor should set an initial temperature. Add a getter getTemperature() (const) and a setter setTemperature() that only allows values between 10 and 30 degrees Celsius. Add a member function display() that shows the temperature with a "Hot", "Cold", or "Comfortable" label (above 25 = Hot, below 18 = Cold, otherwise Comfortable).

Solution:

cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Thermostat {
private:
    double temperature_;
    
public:
    Thermostat(double initialTemp) : temperature_(initialTemp) {}
    
    double getTemperature() const {
        return temperature_;
    }
    
    void setTemperature(double newTemp) {
        if (newTemp >= 10.0 && newTemp <= 30.0) {
            temperature_ = newTemp;
        } else {
            cout << "Temperature " << newTemp << " is out of range (10-30). Setting not changed." << endl;
        }
    }
    
    void display() const {
        cout << "Temperature: " << temperature_ << "°C - ";
        
        if (temperature_ > 25.0) {
            cout << "Hot" << endl;
        } else if (temperature_ < 18.0) {
            cout << "Cold" << endl;
        } else {
            cout << "Comfortable" << endl;
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    Thermostat thermo(22.5);
    thermo.display();              // Temperature: 22.5°C - Comfortable
    
    thermo.setTemperature(28.0);
    thermo.display();              // Temperature: 28°C - Hot
    
    thermo.setTemperature(15.0);
    thermo.display();              // Temperature: 15°C - Cold
    
    thermo.setTemperature(35.0);   // Temperature 35 is out of range (10-30). Setting not changed.
    thermo.display();              // Still 15°C - Cold
    
    return 0;
}


Chapter 4: The this Pointer
How Member Functions Know Which Object to Modify
Every member function in C++ receives a hidden pointer to the 
object it was called on. This pointer is named this. Understanding this 
explains how one function definition can work with many objects.

4.1 The Problem
A class defines member functions once. Many objects exist. 
When alice.deposit(100) is called, how 
does the deposit function know to modify alice's balance, not bob's?

cpp
class BankAccount {
private:
    double balance_;
    
public:
    void deposit(double amount) {
        // Which balance does this modify?
        balance_ = balance_ + amount;
    }
};

int main() {
    BankAccount alice, bob;
    alice.deposit(100);  // Modifies alice's balance
    bob.deposit(50);     // Modifies bob's balance
    
    return 0;
}
The answer is the this pointer.

4.2 The Solution: The this Pointer
When a member function is called, the compiler 
automatically passes a hidden argument: a pointer 
to the object on which the function was called.

cpp
// What the programmer writes
alice.deposit(100);

// What the compiler generates (conceptually)
BankAccount::deposit(&alice, 100);
Inside the member function, this hidden pointer is accessible as this. 
The this pointer always points to the current object.

cpp
class BankAccount {
private:
    double balance_;
    
public:
    void deposit(double amount) {
        // this points to the object being modified
        this->balance_ = this->balance_ + amount;
    }
};
The compiler treats a plain member variable access like balance_ as this->balance_. The two forms are identical.

4.3 Proving this Exists
The address stored in this is the same as the address of the object.

cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Demo {
public:
    void showAddress() {
        cout << "this points to: " << this << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Demo obj1;
    Demo obj2;
    
    cout << "Address of obj1: " << &obj1 << endl;
    obj1.showAddress();
    
    cout << "Address of obj2: " << &obj2 << endl;
    obj2.showAddress();
    
    return 0;
}
Output (addresses will vary):

text
Address of obj1: 0x7ffd1234abc0
this points to: 0x7ffd1234abc0
Address of obj2: 0x7ffd1234abc8
this points to: 0x7ffd1234abc8
The this pointer contains the address of the object. Each call receives a different this value.

4.4 Using this Explicitly
Explicit this is not required most of the time, 
but it is necessary when a parameter name shadows a member variable.

cpp
class Person {
private:
    string name_;
    int age_;
    
public:
    // Problem: Parameter name same as member variable
    void setName(string name) {
        // Which name? The parameter or the member?
        name = name;  // Wrong - assigns parameter to itself
    }
    
    // Solution: Use this to disambiguate
    void setNameCorrect(string name) {
        this->name_ = name;  // this->name_ is the member variable
    }
    
    // Alternative: Different parameter name (also works)
    void setNameAlternative(string newName) {
        name_ = newName;
    }
};
When explicit this is needed:

Scenario	Why
Parameter shadows member	To distinguish this->member from parameter
Returning the object itself	To enable method chaining (see next section)
Passing the current object to another function	To provide a pointer to the current object
4.5 Returning *this from Member Functions
Dereferencing this (using *this) produces a reference to the current object. Returning *this enables method chaining – calling multiple member functions in sequence.

cpp
class Calculator {
private:
    int value_;
    
public:
    Calculator() : value_(0) {}
    
    Calculator& add(int x) {
        value_ = value_ + x;
        return *this;  // Return reference to current object
    }
    
    Calculator& subtract(int x) {
        value_ = value_ - x;
        return *this;
    }
    
    int getValue() const {
        return value_;
    }
};

int main() {
    Calculator calc;
    
    // Traditional style
    calc.add(5);
    calc.subtract(2);
    
    // Method chaining (possible because each function returns *this)
    calc.add(10).subtract(3).add(7);
    
    cout << calc.getValue() << endl;  // 5 - 2 + 10 - 3 + 7 = 17
    
    return 0;
}
How method chaining works:

calc.add(10) returns *this (reference to calc)

That reference calls subtract(3) on the same calc

That returns *this again

That reference calls add(7)

Method chaining is common in libraries like the C++ iostream library 
(cout << x << y << z) and in builder patterns.

4.6 Chapter Summary
this is a hidden pointer available inside every non-static member function

this points to the object on which the member function was called

Member variable access balance_ is equivalent to this->balance_

Explicit this is needed when a parameter names shadows a member variable

*this dereferences the pointer to get the object itself

Returning *this from a member function enables method chaining

this is a pointer, not a reference (cannot change what it points to)

4.7 Check Your Understanding
Question 1: What does this point to inside a member function?

Answer: this points to the object on which the member function was called.

Question 2: Is this a pointer or a reference?

Answer: this is a pointer.

Question 3: When is explicit this-> required?

Answer: When a parameter name shadows a member variable name, or when the current object needs to be passed to another function.

Question 4: What does *this represent?

Answer: *this dereferences the this pointer, giving the actual object (not a pointer to it).

Question 5: How does method chaining work?

Answer: Each member function returns *this (a reference to the current object), allowing sequential calls to be chained with dot operators.

