Chapter 5: Constructors – Deep Dive
Initializing Objects Correctly
Constructors do more than just set initial values. They establish class invariants – conditions that must be true for the object to be valid. This chapter covers the details of proper object initialization.

5.1 Default Constructor
A default constructor is a constructor that can be called with no arguments. It can have no parameters, or all parameters can have default values.

cpp
class Example {
public:
    // Default constructor with no parameters
    Example() {
        cout << "Default constructor" << endl;
    }
};

class Another {
public:
    // Default constructor with default parameter
    Another(int value = 0) {  // Can be called with no arguments
        cout << "Constructed with " << value << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Example e1;        // Calls default constructor
    Example e2 = Example();  // Also calls default constructor
    
    Another a1;        // Calls Another(0)
    Another a2(42);    // Calls Another(42)
    
    return 0;
}
If no constructor is written, the compiler generates a default 
constructor that does nothing.

cpp
class Simple {
    // No constructors written
    int value;
    // Compiler generates: Simple() {} which leaves value uninitialized
};
If any constructor is written, the compiler no longer generates a 
default constructor.

cpp
class WithCustom {
public:
    WithCustom(int x) { ... }
    // No default constructor - compiler does not generate one
};

int main() {
    // WithCustom w;   // ERROR - no default constructor
    WithCustom w(10);  // OK - custom constructor exists
    
    return 0;
}
To explicitly request the compiler-generated default constructor 
while also having custom constructors:

cpp
class Person {
private:
    string name_;
    int age_;
    
public:
    // Explicitly defaulted default constructor
    Person() = default;
    
    // Custom constructor
    Person(string name, int age) : name_(name), age_(age) {}
};

int main() {
    Person p1;                 // Uses =default constructor
    Person p2("Alice", 30);    // Uses custom constructor
    
    return 0;
}
5.2 Parameterized Constructor
A parameterized constructor takes arguments to initialize 
the object with specific values.

cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Book {
private:
    string title_;
    string author_;
    int year_;
    
public:
    // Parameterized constructor
    Book(string title, string author, int year) 
        : title_(title), author_(author), year_(year) {}
    
    void display() {
        cout << title_ << " by " << author_ << " (" << year_ << ")" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Book b1("1984", "George Orwell", 1949);
    Book b2("Dune", "Frank Herbert", 1965);
    
    b1.display();  // 1984 by George Orwell (1949)
    b2.display();  // Dune by Frank Herbert (1965)
    
    return 0;
}
Multiple constructors can exist in the same class through constructor overloading.

cpp
class Rectangle {
private:
    double width_;
    double height_;
    
public:
    // Default - unit square
    Rectangle() : width_(1.0), height_(1.0) {}
    
    // Parameterized - square of side s
    Rectangle(double side) : width_(side), height_(side) {}
    
    // Parameterized - width and height
    Rectangle(double width, double height) : width_(width), height_(height) {}
};

int main() {
    Rectangle r1;           // Calls Rectangle()
    Rectangle r2(5);        // Calls Rectangle(double)
    Rectangle r3(4, 6);     // Calls Rectangle(double, double)
    
    return 0;
}
5.3 Constructor Initializer List
The initializer list is the colon (:) followed 
by comma-separated initializations that runs before the constructor body.

cpp
ClassName(parameters) : member1(value1), member2(value2) {
    // Constructor body
}
Why the initializer list is preferred:

Reason	Explanation
Efficiency	Member variables are initialized directly, 
not default-initialized then assigned
const members	const members must be initialized in the initializer list
Reference members	References must be initialized in the initializer list
No default constructor	If a member has no default constructor, 
it must be initialized here
cpp
class Member {
public:
    Member(int x) { cout << "Member constructed with " << x << endl; }
};

class Container {
private:
    const int constantValue_;
    int& reference_;
    Member member_;
    
public:
    // const and reference MUST be in initializer list
    // member_ must be in initializer list (no default constructor)
    Container(int value, int& externalRef) 
        : constantValue_(value), 
          reference_(externalRef),
          member_(value * 2) {
        // Body can do additional work
    }
};
Order of initialization is determined by the order members are 
declared in the class, not the order in the initializer list.

cpp
class Example {
private:
    int a_;
    int b_;
    
public:
    // Dangerous - b_ is initialized before a_ in memory, regardless of list order
    Example(int x) : b_(x), a_(b_ + 1) {  // BAD: a_ depends on uninitialized b_
        // a_ uses b_ before b_ is initialized
    }
    
    // Correct - order matches declaration
    Example(int x) : a_(x), b_(a_ + 1) {  // GOOD: a_ then b_
    }
};
Rule: Order the initializer list to match the declaration order of member variables.

5.4 Delegating Constructors (C++11)
A constructor can call another constructor of the same class. 
This reduces code duplication.

cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Student {
private:
    string name_;
    int grade_;
    string major_;
    
public:
    // Full constructor
    Student(string name, int grade, string major) 
        : name_(name), grade_(grade), major_(major) {
        cout << "Student created: " << name_ << ", " << major_ << endl;
    }
    
    // Delegating constructor for unspecified major
    Student(string name, int grade) 
        : Student(name, grade, "Undeclared") {  // Delegates to full constructor
        cout << "Major not specified, defaulted to Undeclared" << endl;
    }
    
    // Delegating constructor for default grade
    Student(string name) 
        : Student(name, 0, "Undeclared") {
        cout << "Grade not specified, defaulted to 0" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s1("Alice", 85, "Physics");   // Full constructor
    Student s2("Bob", 90);                // Delegates to full
    Student s3("Charlie");                // Delegates twice
    
    return 0;
}
Output:

text
Student created: Alice, Physics
Student created: Bob, Undeclared
Major not specified, defaulted to Undeclared
Student created: Charlie, Undeclared
Major not specified, defaulted to Undeclared
Grade not specified, defaulted to 0

5.5 Destructor
A destructor is a special member function that runs when an object is destroyed. 
It has the same name as the class with a tilde (~) prefix and no parameters or 
return type.

cpp
class ResourceHolder {
private:
    int* data_;
    
public:
    ResourceHolder(int size) {
        data_ = new int[size];  // Allocate resources
        cout << "Resources allocated" << endl;
    }
    
    ~ResourceHolder() {
        delete[] data_;  // Clean up resources
        cout << "Resources released" << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    {
        ResourceHolder rh(100);  // Constructor runs - resources allocated
        // Use rh...
    }  // rh goes out of scope - destructor runs - resources released
    
    cout << "After the block" << endl;
    
    return 0;
}
Output:

text
Resources allocated
Resources released
After the block


When destructors run:

When a local object goes out of scope (function ends, block ends)

When an object created with new is deleted (advanced)

In reverse order of construction (members first, then base classes, 
then the class itself)

The Rule of Three (preview): If a class has a destructor, 
it likely also needs a copy constructor and copy assignment operator. 
This is covered in later chapters.

5.6 Chapter Summary
A default constructor takes no arguments; the compiler provides one only if no constructor is written

The = default syntax requests the compiler to generate a default constructor

Parameterized constructors initialize objects with specific values

Constructor overloading allows multiple constructors with different parameters

The initializer list (colon syntax) is preferred over assignment in the constructor body

const and reference members must be initialized in the initializer list

Member initialization order follows declaration order, not initializer list order

Delegating constructors call other constructors of the same class (C++11)

Destructors clean up resources; they run when objects are destroyed

5.7 Check Your Understanding
Question 1: What is the difference between a default constructor and a parameterized constructor?

Answer: A default constructor can be called with no arguments. A parameterized constructor requires arguments.

Question 2: What happens if a class has a custom constructor 
and no default constructor, and the code tries to create an 
object with no arguments?

Answer: Compilation error – no matching constructor.

Question 3: Why is the initializer list preferred over assignment in the 
constructor body?

Answer: For efficiency (direct initialization instead of default then assign), 
and for const members and references which cannot be assigned.

Question 4: In what order are member variables initialized?

Answer: In the order they are declared in the class, not the order in the initializer list.

Question 5: What is the syntax for a delegating constructor?

Answer: In the initializer list, call another constructor: : ClassName(parameters)

